---
import Page from "../../layouts/Page.astro";
const toc = ["The TSB lesson", "The clocks", "Where the first hour goes", "A first hour plan", "The real deadline", "Sources"];
---
<Page title="The first hour decides the fine | Relyxus" description="What went wrong at TSB, and what the first sixty minutes of a serious outage need to look like now." path="/articles/the-first-hour-decides-the-fine" tone="light" current="/articles">
  <div class="rd-prog" aria-hidden="true"><i></i></div>
  <article class="k-sec k-white rd"><div class="wrap cols">
    <aside class="toc"><b>Contents</b>{toc.map((t, i) => <a href="#" class={i === 0 ? "on" : ""}>{t}</a>)}</aside>
    <div class="body">
      <span class="k-tag" style="color:#E5482A"><i class="d"></i>Regulation</span>
      <h1 class="k-h1" style="margin-top:14px;font-size:clamp(40px,5vw,68px)">The first hour decides the fine</h1>
      <div class="by"><span class="av">BI</span><div><b>Bisma Idrees</b><small>14 Nov 2026, 8 minute read</small></div></div>
      <p class="lead">Every bank goes down. What regulators now judge is what you did in the first hour, how you told them, and whether you can prove it.</p>
      <p>From 18 March 2027, UK banks must send the FCA and PRA an initial incident report, generally within 24 hours of deciding an incident crosses the threshold. Singapore has asked for a call within one hour since 2014. The EU's DORA wants four hours from classification. American banks have 36. The outage itself is rarely the failure that gets fined. The failure is a slow, confused first hour that turns a bad night into a public story.</p>
      <h2>The TSB lesson</h2>
      <p>In spring 2018, TSB moved about 5 million customers onto a new IT platform as part of its separation from Lloyds. Millions were locked out of their accounts across online, mobile, phone and branch. Problems lasted for weeks, and the bank did not return to business as usual until December.</p>
      <figure class="pull"><b>£48.65m</b><figcaption>The fine the FCA and PRA gave TSB in December 2022, after a roughly five year investigation. The bank had already paid customers £32.7m in redress.</figcaption></figure>
      <p>Read the findings closely and the technology is not the headline. One line matters most for anyone who runs incidents: TSB's early public response understated the scale of the problem.</p>
      <div class="end k-card"><b>See how your own first hour went</b><span>Send us one past incident and we will show you where the minutes went.</span><a class="k-b1" href="/replay">Replay my outage free</a></div>
    </div>
  </div></article>
</Page>
